WEBVTT


00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:05.680
Let's use DesignaKnit to
create a triangular shawl.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:12.560
We'll make the shawl exactly the
size we want and we'll make sure

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:16.010
that we can knit it on our machine
with a number of needles available.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:20.630
We'll also use the yarn
calculation feature to make

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:22.520
sure that we have enough yarn.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.050
In Original Pattern Drafting

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:27.160
start a new file.

00:00:28.215 --> 00:00:33.555
I'm planning to use my LK-150 and I've
already swatched and I know my gauge.

00:00:34.245 --> 00:00:35.715
So I'll enter it now.

00:00:36.254 --> 00:00:37.935
It can always be changed later.

00:00:43.715 --> 00:00:47.075
Click piece, >block new piece.

00:00:48.695 --> 00:00:49.595
Enter a name.

00:00:51.065 --> 00:00:53.915
Now let's size this new
piece to our dimensions.

00:00:54.885 --> 00:00:57.074
Use the >fit to width tool.

00:00:58.214 --> 00:01:03.405
Since my rulers are showing stitches in
rows, it's prompting me with stitches.

00:01:04.185 --> 00:01:07.035
I have 150 needles
available on my machine.

00:01:08.985 --> 00:01:15.285
I want my shawl to be 30 inches long,
so I'll switch the rulers to inches

00:01:16.365 --> 00:01:18.854
and use the >fit to length tool.

00:01:22.024 --> 00:01:25.445
Use the >measure edge
tool to confirm the size.

00:01:31.074 --> 00:01:33.955
Remember, we measure counter-clockwise.

00:01:40.244 --> 00:01:41.384
And here's a tip.

00:01:41.595 --> 00:01:45.524
Click again to turn off
the measuring on one side.

00:01:46.574 --> 00:01:48.405
Let's now make this a triangle.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:52.170
Use the >delete tool, the minus sign.

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:57.810
And since I have >mirroring on,
I just have to remove one point.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:01:59.040
That's it.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:06.880
I have a triangle that I can knit with all
150 needles, but do I have enough yarn?

00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:11.110
Let's use DesignaKnit
to calculate it for us.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.850
Click >file >print.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:27.940
Choose >yarn calculation.

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:35.290
Now, select the >single wrench tool
and tell DesignaKnit about your swatch.

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:39.400
How many stitches and rows
did you knit in your swatch?

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:44.019
How much yarn did you use?

00:02:44.665 --> 00:02:51.065
Weighing in grams with a food
scale is best click okay.

00:02:52.295 --> 00:02:53.805
And then >preview.

00:02:55.265 --> 00:02:59.475
DesignaKnit is telling me how much
yarn I need based on my swatch.

00:03:02.245 --> 00:03:05.045
Next, let's get the knitting instructions.

00:03:06.135 --> 00:03:08.755
You might want to use
Interactive Knitting, but

00:03:08.755 --> 00:03:10.555
that's a topic for another day.

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:14.680
Here's a shortcut you may not have found.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:19.870
Click this button and you can now
choose your preferred printing method.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:26.790
If you use garment notation or Japanese
notation, if you use that format,

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.040
you'll want to align the columns.

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:34.670
Tick this box, it'll
make it easier to read.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:48.680
Now you might be tempted to work your
shaping over every other row, but notice

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:54.440
that DesignaKnit is accurately creating
the shaping to make your desired length.

00:03:58.935 --> 00:04:01.035
I hope this tutorial is helpful.

00:04:01.275 --> 00:04:05.055
The tools you used for this
exercise will help you create

00:04:05.775 --> 00:04:08.145
any other shape and DesignaKnit.

